A||N||14||Carl||Failed Smog in Calif||||9||
Z||000000||Carl||02-27-2001||06:53 PM||||I have a 1987 GT V6 2.8 Automatic that I just purchased. I just got back from the California %$#&$& Smog Check and it failed. The car runs great with no codes showing. The part I failed was the idle test HC ( hydro carbon )<BR>Allowable is 120PPM and I had 194PPM.<P>Can someone get me started down the right path please. All help is appreciated !!||||reg||9
Z||000001||87GTZ34||02-27-2001||08:04 PM||||When was the last time it was tuned up, air filter changed etc.?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ed||02-27-2001||08:18 PM||||Besides the usual tune-up stuff (including checking your catalytic converter), try some of that magic stuff in bottle that's supposed to clean up your engine for the test.  Let us know if it works 'cause my car came within inches of failing the last time, and if it does the next time I'll be looking for help too.  <P>My car is kept up to specs, but I'm afraid its going to fail the next time.  Oh, well, if it fails I guess I'll replace the engine with a 3.4!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carl||02-27-2001||08:55 PM||||Don't know when the last time it was tuned up but I did check the timing after purchase because it was idleing at 1000rpm and I thought it was high.<P>I tried the magic stuff before at $6 per bottle - it did not work.<P>I did change the oil before the test today and as I stated before the car runs like a dream and there are no codes showing.<P>What can make it have a high HC number ?<BR>How do I check the converter ?<BR>If it's running great will a tune-up will a tune-up reduce the HC number ?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||02-28-2001||12:59 AM||||Well i guess running great is relative... It may run better, maybe not.  I would pull a plug or two (or three) and have a look. Also, check the air filter.  These aare certainly easier to check than the cat.  You also may want to put in a good injector cleaner/lube.  If nothing there, then check all vacuum lines, egr operation etc.||||reg||1
Z||000005||862M4inCA||02-28-2001||02:57 AM||||I had a problem with mine not passing smog when I bought it (86 2.5)...got a great deal from a friend and told her I'd take care of the smog.....after taking it in to test it unofficially it failed. I went thru the checklist of things to check, egr, O2, TPS, MAP, etc.....everything checked out fine. I took it in to have it "diagnosed" and they said I had a cracked head (and they call themselves mechanics???), Well $450 later it's got a new one on, still failed.... Checked the Catalytic Converter (which SHOULD have been something they would have checked with the diagnostic) found it to be hollowed out, after my friend had replaced it the year before. Replaced the cat and passed with my HC numbers falling well under 100 (closer to the tune of 5 on the low, and 14 on the high. *GRIN*) Might be something to check out, especially if it's running as well as you say.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Joe4x4||02-28-2001||05:52 AM||||I used GM-Top Engine Clearner, it worked for me. $6<P>------------------<BR>Fiero got fire<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/exp.gif">          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/animated_cat.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Predator||02-28-2001||07:52 AM||||This may sound silly, but was your car up to temp before the test. I had a friend who brought her car in to have checked here in Georgia. The place was right down the street so the car never got up to temp before it was tested and failed. She went to a mechanic, and he told her about the warming up part which the guy who tested it should have thought of. She drove the car around the perimeter before bringing it in and it passed fine. Just a thought. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||02-28-2001||09:51 AM||||Use premium gas, make sure vehicle is hot, Don't turn off car if waiting in line, If waiting in line, give it a couple of revs before going into the test. You can also run some fuel system cleaner thru it to clean out some of the deposits. Check the Catalytic.||||reg||1
Z||000009||JimIndyRCR||02-28-2001||10:24 AM||||Two things: 1st, the smog shops in CA should NOT be checking your idle gas #'s, only 1500 rpm and 2500 RPM on the 'dyno'.  2nd, a high HC is a indicator of a lean condition and a high CO is an indicator of a rich condition- usually when 'we' back the ignition down a couple of degrees at a time  the HC comes down- judging by how close you are to passing I'd bet your cat is ok.  Email me directly and I can help more as I have a shop and this is what we do.<BR>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000010||stsmithgt||02-28-2001||12:34 PM||||I definately check the timing.  Mine failed CA smog because mine was too far advanced.(2.8L).  After setting the timing, it passed with ease.  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||02-28-2001||01:03 PM||||There are still two methods used in California depending on the area. We still use the idle and high RPM test and the two speed test.<P>Where I live it's the idle/high RPM, but down in the valley it's the two speed test. <P>Since you are in Cameron Park, I'll bet it's idle/high RPM. Same as I have in Auburn.<P>Since high RPM is first, that thing must have been closed loop by the time it hit the idle test.<P>Cat's my first choice unless the O2 is dead. Don't run a 180F thermstat, 195F only.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||deceler8||02-28-2001||01:46 PM||||Excessive carbon deposits can result in a high HC number...try a cleaner or use the old-fashioned water cleaning trick...<P>Put your canister purge hose in a container of water, pinch the hose shut, rev to 2000 rpm and slowly unpinch...secret is to closely monitor the water going in and keeping the idle up. The water explodes into steam in the cylinders and loosens carbon.<P>A good carbon cleaner in the can will do the same job, but the water doesn't smoke everyone in your neighborhood out.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fieroshop||03-01-2001||02:52 AM||||When ever I take a car to a smog station and it has high hydro carbons it means 99% of the time it needs a new cat. Before installing a new cat install a new o-2 sensor. This sensor will cause the cat to go out. <BR>I remember a Fiero I had the cat installed and did not replace the o-2 before. Well the short of it was, by the time I got back to the shop, (3 Miles) the new cat was fried.<P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||WKDFIRO||03-02-2001||01:29 PM||||I have to agree with Mr. Miller on this one. Those two items (the O2 sensor and the cat) are the frontlines of your smog check. <P>If the cat isn't too far gone simply replacing the 02 sensor has been known to work. Ive never known a car that couldn't pass with a new cat and new 02 sensor with ridiculously low numbers. On an 87 coupe I had my HC # down to below 50. The welds on the cat weren't even cooled off when I took it into the smog guy!||||reg||1
